essons that incorporate both the standards for mathematical content and the standards for mathematical practice  at the end of this session you will be able to </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6F0BRHGAiKg.6bLiYnWcYcz" slidebank="false">reflect on the discussion in session 9. after posting and reading others’ responses on the discussion board, did you find the intersection of the standards for mathematical content and standards for mathematical practice to be explicit?  #36 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6F0BRHGAiKg.5oEHCkB1WPu" slidebank="false">what are the critical points to remember when planning rich mathematics lessons? as you complete this session, keep the following  big question in mind: #36 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6goI24z7SE6.5Ui0a8kgW5B" slidebank="false">the standards for mathematical content and the standards for mathematical practice are only as powerful as their successful implementation into classroom rituals, routines, and everyday practices. successful implementation requires careful planning.   #36  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6goI24z7SE6.6pGHnueR84o" slidebank="false">the backward design model consists of three steps: (1) determine the desired results or learning goal, (2) decide on acceptable evidence of the result being met, and (3) plan learning activities and instruction to get students to the point where they can successfully complete the task that serves as evidence. supporters of the backward design model maintain that educators should first determine what they want students to know and be able to do and determine how they will know if students reach that learning level. basically, teachers should plan the assessment first, and then they should plan learning activities and instruction with that assessment in mind.   #36 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6goI24z7SE6.6hYQbXBMLQZ" slidebank="false">one highly effective way to plan for actual classroom activities to implement in a rich mathematical lesson is the three-phase lesson structure. the three-phase lesson structure is a problem-based teaching method described in elementary and middle school mathematics: teaching developmentally by john van de walle, karen karp, and jennifer bay-williams (2010, 48).  the lesson structure is divided into three phases: before, during, and after. before during after #36  during the before phase, the teacher sets up the problem, activates students’ prior knowledge without giving away a solution strategy to the current problem, makes sure students understand the problem, and establishes clear expectations for how the students will work the problem (for example, in groups, on their own, verbal responses, written responses, and so on).  before:  in the during phase of the lesson, students work through the assigned problem. while students work, the teacher circulates throughout the room and allows the students to purposefully struggle with the problem in order to create their own strategies and understand the mathematics. the teacher listens actively to determine which students need additional support and provides those students with appropriate hints. the teacher also provides worthwhile extensions for those students who are ready to move on.  during:   in the after phase of the lesson, the teacher brings the students back together and holds a public discussion of the students’ work. during this discussion, students publicly explain their strategies and other students and the teacher ask questions and listen actively without evaluation. this public discussion is used to promote a mathematical community of learners. at the end of the public discussion, the teacher summarizes the main (big) ideas and identifies future problems.  after: </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6mZfOrvZjwC" slidebank="false">establish clear expectations.  lead class discussions on what the problem asks but not a particular solution strategy.  before during after provide appropriate hints and extensions.  have students collaborate on a solution.  identify future problems.  #37 check your answer  correct that is right! you selected the correct responses.  continue  incorrect that is incorrect. please try again. continue  try again incorrect that is incorrect. please try again. see the answers  instructions throughout this institute, you have had the opportunity to examine instructional strategies for implementing the standards for mathematical practice. as you prepare to plan your final lesson, practice building a lesson with these strategies by dragging and dropping them into the part of the lesson where they will have the greatest impact.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.5mSBGK8UThh" slidebank="false">establish clear expectations.  lead class discussions on what the problem asks but not a particular solution strategy.  provide appropriate hints and extensions.  have students collaborate on a solution.  identify future problems.  #37 continue after during before </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6ga9thH60Vz" slidebank="false">clarify the problem.  allow students to struggle with the important mathematics.  actively listen to students’ ideas and explanations.  make connections between  student strategies.  assess the learning goals.  before check your answer during after #37  correct that is right! you selected the correct responses.  continue  incorrect that is incorrect. please try again. continue  try again incorrect that is incorrect. please try again. see the answers </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6NfA1VGZ3Wq" slidebank="false">clarify the problem.  allow students to struggle with the important mathematics.  actively listen to students’ ideas and explanations.  make connections between  student strategies.  assess the learning goals.  continue after during before #37 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.5oArqoo0UpK" slidebank="false">provide a context for the problem. have students publicly present and justify their solution strategies.  summarize main ideas.  activate prior knowledge.  model critical thinking strategies.  before check your answer during after #37  correct that is right! you selected the correct responses.  continue  incorrect that is incorrect. please try again. continue  try again incorrect that is incorrect. please try again. see the answers </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5p6EozilRcj.6XafCQzKMUs" slidebank="false">provide a context for the problem. have students publicly present and justify their solution strategies.  summarize main ideas.  activate prior knowledge.  model critical thinking strategies.  #37 continue after during before </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.6dDpg965fID" slidebank="false">part 1 final project part 2 assessment of learning </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.6kXsDmr5Gje" slidebank="false">use the template to create your final outline for a lesson that includes the standards for mathematical content, the standards for mathematical practice, and the learning activities.  #46-48 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.65sWx2cBVm2" slidebank="false">it is time to show what you have learned in this virtual institute!  please access the assessment of learning through pearson learning studio.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.5dIZmt3bMF6" slidebank="false">reflect on this session. what are the critical points to remember when planning rich mathematics lessons?  #36 </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5xejziefxGz.6rkKyyRTmPr" slidebank="false">congratulations on completing session 10! </slidetext></search></nav_data><resource_data description="&lt;p align='left'&gt;&lt;font face='Arial' size='11' color='#444444'&gt;Here are some useful links and documents:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" /><transcript_data /><glossary_data /></bwFrame>